class:: BlipPower
summary:: Language-side power shaping of a finite Blip kernel
categories:: UGens>Generators>Deterministic
related:: Guides/BlipExtensions, Classes/BlipAD, Classes/BlipDecay, Classes/BlipDiff, Classes/BlipDisp, Classes/BlipFB, Classes/BlipFrac, Classes/BlipInt, Classes/BlipKernel, Classes/BlipPM, Classes/BlipPeak, Classes/BlipSeries, Classes/BlipWidth

description::

BlipPower is a pseudo-UGen composed from link::Classes/BlipKernel:: and
ordinary server operators. Non-finite power input is sanitized and its output
is deterministically masked to zero. Bipolar mode evaluates
code::sign(x) * abs(x).pow(power)::; unipolar mode evaluates
code::abs(x).pow(power)::. A power below one broadens low-level structure, one
is unchanged, and a power above one sharpens the aligned peak.

This first release is deliberately not a native UGen: identical unoversampled
C++ arithmetic would not improve the DSP. Power shaping is nonlinear and
creates harmonics beyond the base kernel, which can alias. No bandlimited or
oversampled claim is made.

The closest vanilla UGen is link::Classes/Blip:: followed by ordinary
code::abs:: and code::pow:: operators. BlipPower packages that graph with
defined zero handling and polarity selection.

classmethods::

method:: ar

argument:: freq
Base frequency in Hz. Audio or control rate; signed values are valid.

argument:: numharm
Rectangular base-kernel harmonic count, sampled once per block and limited by
link::Classes/BlipKernel::.

argument:: power
Audio- or control-rate exponent, clipped to a positive minimum of
code::0.001::.

argument:: polarity
Initialization-rate selector. code::0:: or code::\bipolar:: preserves sign;
code::1:: or code::\unipolar:: returns a nonnegative signal.

argument:: mul
Post-processing multiplier.

argument:: add
Post-processing offset.

examples::

code::
(
{ BlipPower.ar(100, 40, 2, \bipolar, 0.1) }.play;
)
::

code::
(
{ BlipPower.ar(80, 60, SinOsc.ar(0.1).range(0.5, 4), \unipolar, 0.1) }.play;
)
::

code::
// Modulating all parameters for a dynamic sound
(
{ BlipPower.ar(MouseX.kr(50, 400, 'exponential'), MouseY.kr(1, 100), SinOsc.kr(0.2).range(0.5, 3.0), \bipolar) ! 2 * 0.1 }.play;
)
::
